raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Among American X/Twitter users, is hostile political expression associated with the interests, knowledge, capacities, and activities usually understood as signs of democratic engagement?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine hostile expression in political exchanges on X/Twitter and ask whether\nsuch expression is associated with the same forms of engagement that are typically viewed as central to democratic\ncitizenship.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is the association stronger among users who feel more favorably toward their own party’s leaders than toward the opposing party’s leaders?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 2 concerns whether the associations between engagement and hostility are stronger among users with higher\nleader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do users who express more political hostility post more often and have larger potential audiences, or are their voices confined to the margins?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 3. Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.\nWe treat this final expectation as exploratory in nature given the lack of existing research.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Hostile political expression is not confined to people who are marginalized or disengaged. On American X/Twitter, it is positively associated with several characteristics ordinarily valued in democratic citizens.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Who produces hostile expression may help explain its visibility: the people posting it are themselves more active and potentially reach more users, quite apart from any attention their content attracts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This suggests that hostile political\nexpression may be more visible on X/Twitter not only because hostile content attracts attention, but also because users\nwho express such hostility are more active and have greater potential reach [3].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors recruited consenting adults through YouGov’s US panel and linked six survey waves, spanning January 2019 to January 2021, to public X accounts. They linked 2,567 panelists to survey responses; analyses needing only X data included up to 3,643 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "All participants were adults (18 years or older); no minors were included.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "were then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021. The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The behavioral corpus comprised more than nine million posts collected from March 2019 through January 2021. The unit of analysis was the user, not the post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The surveys distinguished background capacities from motivation, knowledge, and behavior. They measured education and perceived political competence, asked about political interest, tested factual knowledge, and recorded self-reported frequencies of online and offline participation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "From the surveys, we obtained individual-level measures of respondents’\neducation, sense of internal efficacy, political knowledge, political interest, self-reported participation in offline settings,\nand self-reported participation in online settings.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Political knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "asking participants on scales ranging from “1 = Never” to “7 = Several times a day”\nhow often they participate in eleven offline political activities",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure online participation, we asked participants on the same response scale how often\nthey engaged in ten online political activities",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary hostility score used word embeddings learned from panelists’ posts. The authors scored each post by its proximity to a combined political-and-hate vector, then averaged scores by user or by user and period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec method [61,62].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector:",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Fourth and finally, we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation included comparison with 2,469 expert-labeled posts; the embedding score achieved 74% held-out accuracy against a 50% baseline. Other checks used AFINN sentiment, Perspective API toxicity, a supervised classifier, and the political and hate components separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The SU approach compared this score to other available text-based\nindicators in our data: a lexicon-based sentiment score (AFINN), Google Perspective API toxicity, and a supervised\nclassifier trained directly on the same expert labels.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility).",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For the main cross-sectional comparisons, the authors pooled observations across waves, clustered standard errors by user, controlled for broad personality and demographic differences and party identification, and estimated missing values.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Not all measures are available in each survey wave and we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random-intercept cross-lagged panel models tested whether changes in a user’s observed online engagement preceded changes in hostility, and vice versa, separately from stable differences between users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "To examine whether the same pattern appears in\ntemporal within-person models, we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarization was the gap between a respondent’s average warmth ratings for three own-party and three opposing-party leaders. The authors compared groups above and below the scale midpoint and estimated interactions with age and gender controls.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer. We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity. The figure illustrates the interaction term from\neach model from the interaction between the engagement indicators and leader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked for false positives from multiple comparisons within four families of tests, using false-discovery-rate corrections and Bonferroni-adjusted intervals.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exploratory negative-binomial models related hostility to three counts: posts, accounts followed, and followers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We investigate the visibility of politically hostile users by first showing results from count models that examine whether\nthose who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A supplementary Danish X/Twitter sample provided a limited comparison: hostility there also correlated positively with political interest, perceived competence, and news consumption.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption,",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Political interest, factual knowledge, and online participation had the strongest positive associations with hostility. The overall pattern persisted after accounting for broad personality traits, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The association was not simply a consequence of engaged users discussing politics more often: adjusting for the political share of each user’s posts slightly strengthened the results, and alternative measures broadly reproduced the pattern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts. This suggests that the results are not simply driven by more politically engaged users posting more about politics.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarized feelings about leaders most clearly strengthened the association of hostility with online and offline participation. Only eight of eighteen interactions survived false-discovery-rate correction; the evidence did not support a general strengthening across all indicators.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show\nthe clearest support for online and offline participation,",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators. The conditional pattern should therefore be treated as exploratory for the remaining indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "When the same user became more politically engaged online, higher hostility tended to follow across all three measures. Evidence that hostility preceded greater engagement was weaker and less consistent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Comparing average hostility with hostility 1.5 standard deviations above average, estimated posts rose from about 420 to 900 and followers from about 220 to 370. The association with the number of accounts followed was smaller.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The main case is a politically polarized US setting on a public, text-based platform. The study does not establish that the same relationships hold in other national settings or on platforms with different audiences, moderation, and interaction norms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data collection focused on the United States specifically because the associations of interest may be especially\nvisible in highly polarized political environments.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "This platform is a public, textbased, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observational panel does not establish that engagement causes hostility. Within-person comparisons address stable differences but leave room for changing confounders, measurement error, and effects in both directions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding\nby focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error. Political sophistication or partisan involvement may be common causes of both participation and hostility.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not establish causal pathways among the engagement indicators. In particular, it does not show that education produces hostility independently of more educated users’ higher engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the present findings are mute on the exact causal relationships between the different indicators of political engagement and their antecedents.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization result is descriptive, not evidence of causal moderation: polarized feelings might precede hostile interactions, accompany them, or result from them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "leader-directed affective\npolarization may be an antecedent, correlate, or consequence of hostile online interaction. We therefore interpret these\nanalyses as suggestive evidence of conditional associations, not as causally identified moderation effects.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The feelings measured were about political leaders; the study cannot say whether feelings about parties themselves or ordinary supporters condition hostility in the same way.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than\ntoward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard [22].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcome excludes nonpolitical abuse but is broader than hate speech. It is not a measure of every kind or target of online hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Even after validation, the automated hostility score may pick up discussion using the vocabulary of political conflict rather than an actual expression of hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measure may partly capture\nproximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not decide between grievance-based and psychological explanations. In particular, controlling for the Big Five does not test explanations involving sadism, the Dark Triad, or other specifically antagonistic traits.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures. Instead, it asks whether online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More posts and followers indicate opportunities to be seen, not demonstrated influence. The descriptive network analysis does not establish centrality or community structure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they\nshould not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "we include exploratory descriptive\nnetwork visualizations of the study participants’ follower/friend graph in S1 Appendix but do not draw inferences about\ncentrality or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors combine the classical account of the capacities and motivation behind democratic participation with revisionist work on motivated reasoning and polarization. They extend the latter from biased political judgment and misinformation sharing to hostile expression online.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "prior research has only to a limited extent applied this revisionist perspective om\npolitical engagement to politics on social media and mainly in the context of misinformation sharing [53]. Here, we extend\nand integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on\nonline political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Rather than treating engagement as a single resource, the authors distinguish what equips people to engage, what motivates or informs them, and what they actually do.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, education and internal efficacy are distal antecedents of political engagement. Second, political interest and political\nknowledge are motivational and cognitive indicators of engagement. Third, online and offline political participation are\nbehavioral indicators of engagement. This distinction follows the theoretical framework above and avoids treating all\nvariables as interchangeable indicators of a single resource construct.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They bring affective polarization into engagement theory as a possible condition: engagement supplies opportunities to participate, while feelings about opponents may shape whether that participation is antagonistic.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Engagement provides attention, motivation, skills, and opportunities to participate in political discussion. Affective polarization may shape whether such participation is expressed in\nantagonistic terms toward political opponents. Under this logic, the association between engagement and hostility should\nbe stronger among users who evaluate opposing political elites more negatively.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Almond and Verba argue that democratic citizenship requires restraint as well as active involvement. The authors build on that qualification to the classical defense of engagement by examining whether political involvement can accompany antagonism rather than respectful debate.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In particular [20], argued that engagement needed to be tempered with a more parochial relationship to politics such that “the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory\ngoals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.”",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "In such settings, politically engaged citizens may use their political competences\nand motivation not to make political exchanges less but more contentious.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
